  <section id="one-command" class="section onecmd-section" aria-labelledby="onecmd-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Backend + admin quick start</p>
          <h2 id="onecmd-title">GoCommerce and its admin on your own server</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-lede">One Docker command starts three services: PostgreSQL, GoCommerce with its admin, and Caddy. It does not start a storefront. This compose file is served from this domain and was run end to end before it was published here.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="onecmd-grid">
        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-head">
            <p><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-terminal"/></svg>Get it and run it</p>
            <button class="copy" data-copy="code-compose-run" data-ev="docker_compose_copy">Copy commands</button>
          </div>
          <pre id="code-compose-run"><code set:html={composeCmd} /></pre>
          <p class="code-note"><a href="/docker-compose.yml" data-ev="compose_downloaded">docker-compose.yml</a> · <a href="/env.example" data-ev="compose_downloaded">env.example</a></p>
        </div>

        <div class="terminal" role="img" aria-label="Terminal output: postgres, gocommerce and web containers started, then a 200 from localhost.">
          <p class="term-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span></p>
          <pre class="term"><code set:html={termOut} /></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub">What runs</h3>
      <ul class="outcome">
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><b>PostgreSQL</b><span>the database GoCommerce owns</span></li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><b>Commerce API</b><span>{counts.apiOperations} documented operations</span></li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><b>Admin panel</b><span>compiled into the same binary</span></li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><b>Reverse proxy</b><span>Caddy, so it answers on port 80</span></li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="sub">What does not</h3>
      <ul class="outcome outcome-not">
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-store"/></svg><b>A storefront</b><span>Svelte Commerce is separate; see below</span></li>
        <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-bot"/></svg><b>AI gateway and MCP</b><span>modules this build does not install</span></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="urls">
        <div class="url"><p class="url-what">Admin</p><p class="url-where"><code>http://localhost/</code></p><p class="url-note">Sign in with the email and password from your <code>.env</code>.</p></div>
        <div class="url"><p class="url-what">API documentation</p><p class="url-where"><code>http://localhost/docs</code></p><p class="url-note">The OpenAPI contract this build actually serves.</p></div>
        <div class="url"><p class="url-what">Catalogue</p><p class="url-where"><code>http://localhost/api/products</code></p><p class="url-note">Public and unauthenticated — the route a storefront calls.</p></div>
      </div>

      <p class="fineprint">
        <b>The storefront is separate:</b> this stack does not pair Svelte Commerce
        with GoCommerce. To do that, install the early GoCommerce connector
        (<code>@misiki/gocommerce-connector</code>, {counts.connectorVersion}) in a Svelte Commerce checkout
        and point it at this stack. In a test, {counts.connectorTested} worked end to
        end; sign-in, the address book and order history need GoCommerce’s identity module, which this stack does not install. <a href="/gocommerce-svelte-commerce-connector/">See the connector status</a>.
        GoCommerce builds from source on first run, because no image is published yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

